     "type": "discover",
     "topic": "La pasiva en todos los tiempos",
     "intro": "Las noticias usan mucho la pasiva. Mira el verbo be en cada frase.",
     "examples": [
      {
       "en": "The road is being repaired.",
       "es": "Están reparando la carretera.",
       "mark": [
        "is being repaired"
       ]
      },
      {
       "en": "Ten people have been injured.",
       "es": "Diez personas han resultado heridas.",
       "mark": [
        "have been injured"
       ]
      },
      {
       "en": "The suspect was arrested last night.",
       "es": "El sospechoso fue arrestado anoche.",
       "mark": [
        "was arrested"
       ]
      },
      {
       "en": "The results will be announced tomorrow.",
       "es": "Los resultados se anunciarán mañana.",
       "mark": [
        "will be announced"
       ]
      },
      {
       "en": "The bridge had been closed before the storm.",
       "es": "El puente había sido cerrado antes de la tormenta.",
       "mark": [
        "had been closed"
       ]
      },
      {
       "en": "It is believed that the fire started in the kitchen.",
       "es": "Se cree que el incendio empezó en la cocina.",
       "mark": [
        "It is believed"
       ]
      }
     ],
     "question": "¿Qué cambia entre una pasiva y otra?",
     "options": [
      "El tiempo del verbo be (is being, has been, will be…)",
      "El participio",
      "Nada"
     ],
     "answer": 0,
     "rule": "La pasiva es siempre be + participio, y solo cambia el tiempo de be: is done, is being done, was done, has been done, will be done, had been done. Las noticias usan formas impersonales para informar sin afirmar: It is said / believed / reported that… o He is said to be… (se dice que él es…).",
     "ruleExamples": [
      {
       "en": "The minister is reported to have resigned, but it hasn't been confirmed yet.",
       "es": "Según informes, el ministro renunció, pero aún no se ha confirmado."
      }
